All pairs except one express well-known logical equivalences:
1 and 2 are De Morgan laws;
3 is contraposition;
4 is material implication;
6 is the negation of an implication.
Truth-table inspection (see research summary) shows each of these pairs matches in every row. 
For pair 5, however, the truth table differs when A is false: (¬A → B) is true while (A ∨ ¬B) can be false, so the biconditional is not a tautology. Therefore pair 5 is the only statement not equivalent to the others.
All equalities except the fifth have matching truth tables (by De Morgan’s laws, contraposition, material implication, and negation of implication). The table for (¬A → B) and (A ∨ ¬B) differs in two rows, so that biconditional is false, making the whole statement non-equivalent to the others.
